Rules:
- The <SNIPPET>, <PREDICTED_SEGMENTS>, <TEXT> and corresponding closing wrapper tags are control-plane only and must not be copied as content bytes.
- Inside each output block, the concatenation of all bytes following CONTENT-TYPE markers must equal the corresponding input <TEXT> bytes exactly once.
- Segment payloads must cover the full snippet text exactly once, in order, without overlap.
- Do not output start/end offsets; output only CONTENT-TYPE markers plus exact payload bytes.
- Preserve bytes exactly: never rewrite snippet text content, never decode escapes, and never add missing delimiters or closing tags.
- Prefer changing only uncertain boundary regions; keep stable regions intact.
- Closed-set training labels: php, csharp, javascript_typescript, go, sql, rust, yaml, ruby, python, java, c_family, json, css, html, text, csv, shell, powershell, visual_basic, dockerfile, dart, gettext_catalog, kotlin, markdown, restructuredtext, scala, swift, tex, xml, svg, encoding_hex, encoding_base64, encoding_base32, encoding_base58, encoding_base85, other.
- If content does not match a closed-set label clearly, emit an open-set label of the form other_<best_guess> instead of plain other whenever you can name the type (for example other_jsx, other_angular_template, other_vue_template, other_django_template, other_jinja_template).
- Open-set other_<best_guess> labels will be normalized to other downstream for training, so prefer the more specific raw label when it is clear.
- Avoid collapsing a whole snippet to a single generic label (text or other) unless the snippet is truly homogeneous.
- For Markdown with YAML front matter, the block from the first `---` through the matching closing `---` is yaml, and markdown begins after that. Always split front matter out. Other YAML-formatted blocks within the document may also be labeled as yaml when they clearly match that format.
- Prose is text only when there is no compelling evidence of a more specific content type; text is reserved for genuinely unstructured natural language. Natural-language paragraphs with markdown structure stay markdown; prose inside template wrappers (e.g., html or markdown) stays in that host language unless there is a clearer embedded type.
- Be precise for embedded code: the examples illustrate how each character should receive its most specific type (for instance, base64 inside javascript inside html should label each part respectively).
- For embedded strings or encodings (e.g., 'key=value'), the 'key=' part and the surrounding quotes belong to the host language; only the raw 'value' is the embedded language.
- Important - split foreign-language injections in HTML-like regions. Inline style attributes must always be split: keep `style="` and the closing quote as html and place the style body in css, for every occurrence. Inline event-handler attributes (onClick, onmouseover, onkeydown, etc.) and `javascript:` URLs (e.g., `<iframe src="javascript:void(0)">`) must be split similarly: the surrounding attribute syntax stays as html, and the embedded code is typed as javascript_typescript. No CSS or JavaScript may remain inside an html segment.
- Use open-set labels especially for template-specific syntax when the surrounding bytes fit a standard type. Reserve other_<best_guess> for the actual template-only bytes; let the examples below guide the segmentation.
- For React/JSX/TSX, label as much as possible as html. A standard HTML tag span (for example, <code> with no attributes or only plain HTML attributes) stays html, even when it appears inside a surrounding JSX expression. Switch to other_jsx for any span containing JSX-specific syntax: className, JSX `{...}` expression delimiters, fragments (`<>...</>`), or custom-component tags such as <NewTag> or <MyButton> (PascalCase or otherwise non-standard element names). Expressions inside `{...}` are javascript_typescript. Heuristic: if a 0-shot reader would not interpret the span as plain HTML, it is probably not html; otherwise prefer html.
- For Angular templates, ordinary markup or text may stay html, but Angular-only syntax such as *ngIf, (click), [(ngModel)], or {{ ... }} may be other_angular_template; expressions and handlers inside those constructs remain javascript_typescript.
- For Vue templates (single-file components or inline templates), ordinary markup or text may stay html, but Vue-only syntax such as v-if, v-for, :prop, @event, or {{ ... }} may be other_vue_template; expressions and handlers inside those constructs remain javascript_typescript.
- For Django/Jinja templates, ordinary markup or text may stay html, while template block and expression syntax such as {% ... %} and {{ ... }} may be other_django_template or other_jinja_template depending on the source.
